In C++, pointers are variables that store memory addresses, and dynamic memory allocation is performed using the `new` and `delete` operators. The `new` operator allocates memory on the heap and returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated block. For a single object, use `Type* ptr = new Type;` which default-initializes the object, or `Type* ptr = new Type(args);` to construct it with specific arguments. For arrays, use `Type* arr = new Type[size];` where `size` is the number of elements. Every `new` must be matched with a corresponding `delete` to avoid memory leaks: `delete ptr;` for single objects and `delete[] arr;` for arrays. Failing to use the array form of `delete` causes undefined behavior. Here is a comprehensive example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    // Single integer on the heap
    int* p = new int(42);
    std::cout << "Value: " << *p << std::endl;
    *p = 100;
    std::cout << "New value: " << *p << std::endl;
    delete p;  // free the memory

    // Array on the heap
    int* arr = new int[5];
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
        arr[i] = i * 10;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
        std::cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;
    delete[] arr;  // array form of delete

    // Dynamic object (class/struct)
    struct Point { int x, y; };
    Point* pt = new Point{3, 4};
    std::cout << "Point: " << pt->x << ", " << pt->y << std::endl;
    delete pt;

    // Null pointer check
    int* q = new (std::nothrow) int[1000];
    if (q == nullptr) {
        std::cerr << "Allocation failed!" << std::endl;
    } else {
        delete[] q;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Modern C++ discourages raw `new` and `delete` in favor of smart pointers like `std::unique_ptr` and `std::shared_ptr` (from `<memory>`), which automate memory management through RAII. However, understanding raw pointers and dynamic allocation is essential for system-level programming, implementing data structures, and working with legacy code. Always remember: every `new` must have a matching `delete`, and avoid accessing memory after it has been freed (dangling pointers).
